\f1\fs22 Hayley had backed out at Rickenbacker.\
Had she kept going, she would have discovered that quake-trance began before anybody had gods for it, with a warehouse, a DJ and an ugly road case on castor wheels.\
The road case belonged to the DJ. Roadies could move speaker stacks, lighting rigs, power distribution and anything else likely to crush them if dropped from sufficient height, but the case was positioned by the DJ himself. He would have it rolled close to the bass cabinets, shift it a little, stand back, listen, then shift it again. Nobody opened it. Nobody was invited to ask.\
At first, people assumed it contained something to do with the sound system.\
This was accurate without being especially informative.\
Early quake-trance was still recognisably descended from psychedelic trance: long sets, rolling bass, a beat sufficiently regular to reassure people who had misplaced several hours, and enough electronic ornamentation to make the inside of the average head feel professionally decorated. What distinguished it happened underneath.\
The first people to notice it reported pressure in their ears without hearing anything. Others felt it through their feet or teeth. Concrete floors seemed to hum independently of the speakers. Lights phase in and out. Ravers commonly reported feeling like time is slowing down, accompanied by a gentle rhythmic pressure in the ears that can\'92t be heard.\
The usual explanation was drugs, which did not account for the concrete.\
Then buildings began taking an interest.\
At one warehouse the floor developed a vibration that continued several seconds after the bass had stopped. At another, dust came down from roof trusses in time with the music and an engineer subsequently used the phrase 
\f2\i progressive structural distress
\f1\i0 , which proved less alarming to the scene than he had intended. Enough venues were condemned, inspected or suddenly unavailable for private hire that promoters began looking for places where structural damage would be harder to invoice.\
The desert had several.\
Scene lore that quake-trance made the earth move spread faster than a crack in a warehouse wall.\
There was hardly a vid post where someone wasn\'92t trying to explain the \'93drop.\'94 One sludge reel, 
\f2\i How quake-trance could kill you
\f1\i0 , featured a raver in a hospital bed vowing they\'92d be straight back to the canyons once their bones had mended. Others circulated under titles including 
\f2\i When is a drop not a drop
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i Turn left at the burnt sofa
\f1\i0 .\
Unlike more survivable variants of electronic dance music, in which the drop was the moment where the bass came crashing back, a quake-drop is the moment when the surroundings start to participate.\
In a warehouse the floor might hum.\
Out in a dry canyon, the cliff starts humming.\
The ground feels like a carpet thrown over a plague of snakes.\
Dry rock turned out to be particularly good company. It did not absorb much ambiguity. A shift in the music could send loose stones ticking down a slope, shake dust from a fissure or bring a low note out of a wall that had not been invited to the rave.\
The DJ learned.\
He learned where to put the Ark, as the road case had by then become known, and how far it wanted to sit from the bass cabinets. He learned which long, low structures in the music brought a response from exposed rock and which merely loosened dental work. He learned to pay attention when the ground began answering somewhere other than directly beneath the speakers.\
At one desert party a canyon wall began humming strongly enough that people stopped dancing and went over to put their hands against it.\
The sensible response would have been to shut everything down.\
The DJ altered the music.\
The note in the wall changed.\
People felt it through their palms.\
Someone claimed the canyon was breathing. Someone else claimed it had spoken.\
Nobody could later agree on where the party had been, although the surviving invitation was unusually precise:\
DRIVE EAST UNTIL YOUR NAV SAYS, \'93ROAD ENDS IN -10 MILES.\'94\
TURN LEFT AT THE BURNT SOFA.\
Within six months there were T-shirts.\
The names came later, initially as jokes.\
A particularly violent beat was Ludwig. Metallic noises and sudden cracks belonged to Zildjian. Anything that arrived through the soles of your feet, or seemed to be happening somewhere under the visible world, was blamed on Rickenbacker.\
\'93Rickenbacker\'92s awake tonight,\'94 a DJ might say when the ground began moving properly.\
People laughed.\
They kept saying it.\
The joke lasted longer than some of the people who remembered it was a joke.\
Ludwig was said to preside over beat and thunder-cracks. Zildjian spoke it metallic hisses, rides and crashes, while Rickenbacker grumbled with rolling, subterranean frequencies that were said to exist in the spaces between all other sound, both terrestrial and celestial.\
The Ark helped.\
It remained obstinately unimpressive: a road case on wheels which the DJ guarded more closely each year. People knew something inside it spun. The DJ occasionally referred to the three Platters and offered no further technical assistance. By the time anybody thought to ask what a Platter actually was, each had acquired a theological association and the question had become slightly rude.\
Nobody outside the DJ\'92s confidence saw inside.\
This was excellent for religion.\
Repeated jokes became stories about previous Drops. Stories acquired witnesses who had not been born when the events occurred. Particular ways of arranging the sound system became traditions, then observances, then things which had always been done because Ludwig disliked having the left bass stack too close to the Ark.\
Eventually a collection of purists began complaining that quake-trance had lost touch with its spiritual foundations.\
They formed the Order of the Perpetual Drop.\
Most of the scene assumed this was performance art.\
The Order regarded the assumption as persecution and became much more serious.\
Quake-trance was becoming successful enough to deserve the trouble. Commercial promoters could reproduce the beat, the bass, the lights and a thoroughly convincing amount of floor movement. They could advertise a Drop at 8.15, another at 9.30 and a final communion with the Earth at 11 before the buses left.\
What they could not do was make a canyon answer.\
There was only one Ark.\
This inconvenience gradually divided quake-trance into events attended by people who wanted to dance and events attended by people who wanted a geological feature to acknowledge them personally.\
The former made considerably more money.\
Resorts appeared. Desert retreats offered carefully managed quake experiences with no dangerous ground movement whatsoever. Hidden transducers shook floors. Insurance became available. Customers could book spiritual awakening around dinner.\
The Order became appalled enough to declare the desert 
\f3\b spiritually overdeveloped
\f1\b0 .\
They went looking for somewhere less commercial.\
What they found was a bankrupt trance ashram on the coast of Goa.\
Its previous proprietors had promoted a balance of mind, body, spirit and cryptocurrency until their crypto-yogi threw out the disk drive containing the private key to most of the cryptocurrency, having mistaken it for a collection of recordings of the discredited Neo-Hindu Big Sur Vedas.\
The balance deteriorated quickly after that.\
The Order took over the site.\
Nobody involved knew that one of the remote ancestors of their music had itself descended from Goa trance. That ancestry had disappeared into enough centuries, genres, revivals and cultural amnesia that the Order congratulated itself on discovering Goa as an entirely new spiritual environment.\
They brought the Ark with them.\
The American desert had taught quake-trance on dry rock. Dry rock was legible. If the DJ changed something, a nearby cliff might change with it. Dust fell. Stones moved. A wall hummed. The response had edges.\
Goa was wet.\
Rain had worked into the laterite. Groundwater sat underneath it. Mud joined groundwater to creeks and estuaries, and the estuaries eventually ran out of excuses and joined the Arabian Sea.\
The first Goan Drops behaved differently.\
Vibrations seemed slower and deeper. A response might appear well away from the sound system. Palms shivered when the air around them was still. Concentric ripples crossed standing water without agreeing on a centre. Once, a disturbance travelled along an estuary in a direction everybody present knew the tide was not going.\
The DJ adjusted the music and kept notes.\
The Order developed theology.\
Rickenbacker, it was generally agreed, was more communicative in the tropics.\
Wet Quake-Trance followed.\
The services grew larger. The Ark was positioned with increasing care among the bass equipment and the congregation became accustomed to the idea that the most successful Drops were those in which the response could no longer be conveniently assigned to anything they could see.\
One night several thousand people gathered on the site.\
The bass settled into its long pulse. The Ark was running. The ground began to answer, then the water.\
People felt the pressure in their ears.\
The palms moved.\
Ripples formed where nobody had stepped.\
Somewhere beyond the crowd, the estuary developed ideas of its own.\
The DJ kept playing.\
For the Order of the Perpetual Drop, it was shaping up to be an exceptionally good service.\

